    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Formula for the Sum of a Geometric Series Given an Arbitrary Term and the Common Ratio",
    "problem": "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $3$rd term is $4$ and the common ratio is $2?$",
    "worked_solution": "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine ${a}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\ 4 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{(3-1)} \\\\ 4 & =4{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=1$ and $r=2.$ Substituting our values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{1(1-{2}^{N})}{1-2} \\\\  & =-(1-{2}^{N}) \\\\  & ={2}^{N}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
